WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.500
Did you know that some of the top-grossing medications on the market today only actually

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.800
help between 1 in 25 and 1 in 4 of the people taking them?

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.700
This happens because we design our medicines for a mathematical average.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.200
We calculate what works for a broad population and prescribe that middle-ground dose to

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.300
everyone.

6
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.600
The problem is, an entirely average person does not actually exist.

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.300
Look at this scatterplot of patient responses.

8
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.400
Individual lines represent the average result for the placebo group and the treated group.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.400
On paper, the treatment line looks like a success.

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.900
But look at the individual dots scattered everywhere.

11
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.000
Many people taking the medication see no meaningful change at all.

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.700
When we build a medical system that treats a mathematical average, we guarantee that we

13
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.400
are going to miss the actual individual sitting in the doctor's office.

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.300
Right now, standard medicine relies on a static snapshot.

15
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.400
Your doctor takes your vitals during a short clinic visit and uses that single moment in

16
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.500
time to set a fixed dose for you to take over the next few weeks or months.

17
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.100
Imagine trying to navigate rush hour city traffic using a paper map printed 5 years

18
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.100
ago.

19
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.300
You would constantly hit roadblocks because your static map doesn't know about current

20
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.600
construction or accidents.

21
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.900
Human biology works the exact same way.

22
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.800
Your body is not a fixed piece of paper.

23
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.300
It fluctuates constantly throughout the day, across different daily rhythms, hormonal cycles,

24
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.200
and inflammatory responses.

25
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.800
A static dose quickly drifts completely out of alignment with what your body actually needs

26
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.400
at that moment.

27
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:43.100
To successfully treat a moving biological target, medicine requires a live GPS navigation

28
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.000
system, not a static paper map.

29
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.500
That live navigation system is called a biological digital twin.

30
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.800
It functions as a living, continuously evolving computational model of your specific biology,

31
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.200
grounded entirely in your real-world medical data rather than science fiction concepts

32
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.200
of digital cloning.

33
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.500
It starts with a broad baseline map of how a population responds to a treatment.

34
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.000
Then that map is warped and adjusted to perfectly fit your unique genetics, your metabolism,

35
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.900
in your personal clinical history.

36
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:23.200
By doing this, the system translates an abstract study of 1,000 strangers into a deeply personal,

37
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.000
highly accurate profile of you.

38
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.500
To keep that model alive, you wear a biosensor, like a smartwatch or ring, that constantly

39
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.000
streams your real-time physical signs back to the twin as you go about your normal day.

40
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.700
Look at the patient digital twin and delivery panel.

41
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.600
The system compares the expected biological path against what your wearable measures.

42
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:51.000
When a gap opens, it recalculates your next dose, like GPS rerouting traffic.

43
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.400
But it lacks unchecked control.

44
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.800
Notice the cryptographic safety envelope on the left.

45
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.200
This is an authorization envelope.

46
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.600
Your human doctor signs off on strict minimum and maximum limits for your medication.

47
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.100
The software is only allowed to drive safely inside those pre-approved boundaries.

48
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.900
This creates a closed-loop system.

49
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.400
You receive highly responsive, bespoke healing tailored to your exact physical state, while

50
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.900
your physician remains entirely in the driver's seat.

51
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.200
The benefits of this approach extend far beyond your own health.

52
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.700
Every time your digital twin learns from how you respond in the real world, it sends that

53
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.400
evidence back to the researchers discovering new medicines.

54
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.400
When you group many of these individual digital models together, you form a virtual cohort.

55
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.200
This lets researchers test proposed treatments against a wide variety of unique biological

56
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.700
profiles simultaneously.

57
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.400
By running simulations through this virtual network first, researchers can compute the

58
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:59.500
exact spread of individual responses before a physical clinical trial begins.

59
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:04.400
They can identify exactly who a drug will help, and who it won't, before a single

60
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.300
patient takes a pill, rather than discovering those failures years down the line.

61
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:15.100
This is a form of medicine that tracks your individual response, speaks your native biological

62
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.700
language, and learns continuously with you.

63
00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:24.500
We no longer have to base our healthcare on an imaginary, mathematical middle ground.

64
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:30.300
As this technical summary states, digital twins represent the end of average medicine.

65
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.500
By tracking and adapting to your exact physical state, we can finally build a treatment plan

66
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.200
made specifically for you.

end
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.000
Medicine Made By the Body, For the Body. www.instructionalbiology.com
